      "writtenOn": "2021-09-07T19:30:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Even if this did get fixed later (which is doubtful) its existence would badly influence the \"meaty\" parts of the API.\n\nThe parts we\u0027re trying to focus on are Font consumers, it would be pretty weird if they\u0027re impacted by changes in how we build Fonts.\n\n\u003e This should just take a non-nullable typeface handle and then the testing code can create something and pass it in as needed.\n\nIs the typeface abstraction required or particularly useful for high level clients?  I.e. what do we lose if we go directly FontManager -\u003e Font?",
      "parentUuid": "29c4a73e_8e5f784a",
      "revId": "933271d87199ae9079d658d9d7712168243d19cc",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f58276ab_34f56fcf",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5140
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-07T20:09:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What is really wanted is a way for the user to provide a callback thingy which is given the state of shaping after a shaping call and can produce the next \u0027Font\u0027 object to use to shape (and incidentally to font selection also state what ranges should be re-shaped). Unfortunately, the current shaping API is also not great, so not forcing you to write something correct.\n\nTypeface objects should come from a font manager, which will provide some means of matching typefaces, one criteria which may be a family name but may also include style name, the family name in multiple languages, weight, width, slant (these three possibly as ranges), italic, monospace, language, character coverage, color support, axis tag support, feature support, etc.\n\nIn general I would have nothing against going from FontManager -\u003e Font, though then one would need to expose a Typeface object to get off of the Font or expose all the interesting bits of SkTypeface on the Font.\n\nThat being said, most of the issue I have here is about pretending that one can create a Font from a String (assumed to be a family name). This is pernicious and a common misconception (it\u0027s what people think they\u0027re doing when they select a font name from a drop down, or what they think they\u0027re doing when writing a css font-family list) but it isn\u0027t how things work. There is actually a really complicated and policy heavy set of things going on in these cases which requires full font management.",
      "parentUuid": "fc23746d_76f890ad",
      "revId": "933271d87199ae9079d658d9d7712168243d19cc",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
